{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqh2njkvhda","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"Pluggable LLM backend supports local CLIs AND API/OpenRouter; API path promoted to first-class because antivirus flags hidden local-CLI spawns; warn on local-CLI selection","summary":"Supersedes the earlier agentic-CLI-only-first plan. New driver: Kaspersky (behavioral antivirus) intercepts the daemon's hidden (windowsHide) headless claude -p child processes — a background service spawning hidden network-active children is a classic AV malware heuristic. So our popup fix (windowsHide) traded annoying popups for AV interception, and AV may silently kill the spawns (failing the drains). UPDATED DIRECTION for the pluggable, dashboard-selectable LLM engine: support BOTH (a) LOCAL CLIs (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor) via the spawn-a-self-driving-agent model, AND (b) API-key / OpenRouter via a daemon-driven loop that calls the LLM API IN-PROCESS over HTTP — no child process at all. The API path is now FIRST-CLASS (not phase 2) precisely because spawning no process sidesteps BOTH the popup and the AV-interception problems. REQUIREMENT (user point 1): when a user selects a LOCAL-CLI backend (e.g. their local Claude Code), the dashboard MUST surface an awareness message — local CLI drains spawn hidden child processes that antivirus like Kaspersky may flag/block, so they should add the install dir + node + claude binaries to the AV trusted/exclusion list (or choose an API backend to avoid spawning entirely). Architecture unchanged otherwise: lib/llm-backend.js adapter + registry (provider kind 'agentic-cli' | 'api'); generalize lib/claude-cli.js into the Claude provider; route the drains through the selected backend; config in overlay.config.backend via GET/POST /config/backend; dashboard settings panel with per-provider install/auth status + selector + the local-CLI AV warning; hard-block when no valid backend configured. Fire-rate backoff (committed 875e8c1) is already done.","created_by":"main","v